'''3†.''' Without a parallel; above or beyond all others; unique.
{{smaller block|To her, whose worth makes other worthies nothing;<br>She is ''alone.''{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., ii. 4.}}
I am ''alone'' the villain of the earth.{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., iv. 6.}}}}

'''4†.''' Devoid; destitute.
{{smaller block|<poem>For bothe a wydowe was she and ''allone''
Of ony frend to whom she dorst hire mone.</poem>{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, i. 98.}}}}

'''To let alone.''' {{smaller|See ''let.'' ='''Syn.''' ''Alone, Only.'' The attributive use of ''alone'' is now very rare. In the Bible and earlier English ''alone'' is often used for the adverb ''only'', but it is now becoming restricted to its own sense of solitary, unaccompanied by other persons or things.}}
{{smaller block|Who can forgive sins but God ''alone?''{{right|Luke v. 21.}}
Not ''alone'' at Ephesus, but almost throughout all Asia.{{right|Acts xix. 26.}}}}
{{smaller block|In each of these examples ''only'' would now be considered better, though ''not alone'' for ''not only'' is in common use. ''Alone'' means unaccompanied: as, he stood ''alone.'' ''Only'' applies to that of which there is no other: as, an ''only'' son; adverbially, ''only'' this.}}
{{smaller block|And I ''only'' am escaped ''alone'' to tell thee.{{right|Job i. 15.}}}}

'''alonely†''' (a̤-lōn′li), ''adv.'' and ''a.'' [< ME. ''aloonly, alonly'', usually separated, ''al only, all only, al onli, al oonly'', etc.: ''al, all'', adv.; ''only'', adv. Cf. ''alone, allenarly.'' In mod. use abbrev. ''lonely'', esp. as attrib. adj.] '''I.''' ''adv.'' Only; merely; singly.
{{smaller block|This said spirit was not given ''alonely'' unto him, but unto all his heirs and posterity.{{right|''Latimer.''}}
Farewell with him [the medical attendant] all that made sickness pompous—the spell that hushed the household, . . . the sole and single eye of distemper ''alonely'' fixed upon itself.{{right|''Lamb'', Elia, p. 311.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Exclusive; sole; only.

readings.c1s3:
2. In a line, or with a progressive motion; onward: as, let us walk ''along''.
{{smaller block|A firebrand carried ''along'' leaveth a train.
{{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist.}}}}
'''3.''' In company; together.
{{smaller block|<poem>He to England shall [go] ''along'' with you.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|The queen took her leave of Say’s Court, having brought confusion ''along'' with her, and leaving doubt and apprehension behind.
{{right|''Scott'', Kenilworth, I. xv.}}}}
{{smaller|[In this sense it is often used absolutely in common speech in the United States: as, I was not ''along''.]}}—'''All along.''' {{smaller|See ''all''.}}

'''along<sup>2</sup>''' (a-lông′), ''prep.'' [Also abbrev. ''long'' (see ''long''<sup>3</sup>); < ME. ''along'', ''ilong'', < AS. ''gelang'' (= OS. ''gelang'' = OHG. ''gilang''), adj., belonging, depending (with prep. ''on'', on, or ''æt'', at), lit. in line with, in connection with, < ''ge-'', generalizing prefix, + ''lang'', long: see ''ge-'', ''a-''<sup>6</sup>, and ''long''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''belong''.] Owing to; on account of: with ''of'', formerly with ''on''.
{{smaller block|<poem>I can nat telle wher''on'' it was ''along'' [var. ''long''],
But wel I wot greet stryf is vs among.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Yeoman’s Tale, l. 377.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>’Tis all ''along of'' you that I am thus haunted.</poem>
{{right|''H. Brooke'', Fool of Quality, II. 88.}}}}
{{smaller block|All ''along of'' the accursed gold.
{{right|''Scott.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Lady Magdalen.'' Unhappiest
Of Queens and wives and women.
''Alice.'' And all ''along''
''Of'' Philip.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Queen Mary, v. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|[This preposition is now always followed by ''of'', and its use is mainly confined to colloquial or dialectal speech.]}}

