'''aloof''' (a̤-löf′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' and ''prep.'' [Early mod. E. ''aloofe'', ''aloufe'', ''a loofe'', ''a luf''; < ''a''<sup>3</sup>, on, + ''loof'', < D. ''loef'', loof, luff; cf. D. ''te loef'', to loof, i. e., to windward; ''loef houden'', lit. hold loof, keep to the windward: cf. the E. phrase ''to hold aloof''. See ''loof''<sup>2</sup>, ''luff''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''adv.'' At a distance, but within view; intentionally remaining apart, literally or figuratively; withdrawn.
{{smaller block|It is necessary the Queen join, for if she stand ''aloof'' there will be still suspicions. {{right|''Suckling.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Aloof'' he sits
And sullen, and has pitched his tents apart.</poem>
{{right|''M. Arnold'', Sohrab and Rustum.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thy smile and frown are not ''aloof''
From one another;
Each to each is dearest brother.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Madeline.}}}}

'''II.''' ''prep.'' At or to a distance from; away or apart from. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>The great luminary,
''Aloof'' the vulgar constellations thick,
That from his lordly eye keep distance due,
Dispenses light from far.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iii. 577.}}}}

'''aloofness''' (a̤-löf′nes), ''n.'' The state of being aloof, or of keeping at a distance; indifference.
{{smaller block|Unfaithfulness and ''aloofness'' of such as have been greatest friends. {{right|''D. Rogers'', Naaman, p. 93.}}}}
{{smaller block|By the wary independence and ''aloofness'' of his [the Indian’s] dim forest life he preserves his intercourse with his native gods.
{{right|''Thoreau'', Concord and Merrimac Rivers, p. 59.}}}}

