'''alow'''<sup>2</sup> (a-lou′), ''adv.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''low''<sup>4</sup>, fire: see ''low''<sup>4</sup>.] Afire; in a flame. [Scotch.]—{{smaller|'''To gang alow''', to take fire, or be set on fire; blaze; be burned.}}
{{smaller block|That discreet man Cardinal Beaton is e’en to ''gang alowe'' this blessed day if we dinna stop it. {{right|''Tennant.''}}}}

'''alp'''<sup>1</sup> (alp), ''n.'' [< ME. ''alpe''. In Norfolk (England) the bullfinch is called ''blood-olph'', and the green grosbeak ''green-olf'', where ''olph'', ''olf'', may be the same as ''alp''; cf. ''ouphe'' and the other forms of ''elf'', q. v. Possibly a humorous use, with a similar allusion to that in ''bullfinch'', of ME. ''alp'', ''elp'', < AS. ''elp'', ''ylp'', an elephant, < L. ''elephas'': see ''elephant''.] An old local name for the bullfinch, ''Pyrrhula vulgaris''.
{{smaller block|''Alpes'', fynches, and wodewales.
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 658.}}}}

